WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.656
Picture a café on a busy morning.

2
00:00:04.656 --> 00:00:10.820
You tap your card, and the card reader sends a message about the payment to the company that records it.

3
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:15.152
The reader hears nothing back, so it sends the same message again.

4
00:00:15.152 --> 00:00:17.565
Both copies arrive a millisecond apart.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:24.165
The company runs two copies of its program, called workers, so one can carry on if the other crashes.

6
00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:29.333
Each worker picks up one copy, and asks the database: do we have this payment yet?

7
00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:30.626
Each hears no.

8
00:00:30.626 --> 00:00:31.761
Each stores it.

9
00:00:31.761 --> 00:00:34.259
You've paid for one coffee twice.

10
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.940
Looking before you write felt careful.

11
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.417
So how did it go wrong?

12
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.476
First, how can a worker tell a repeat from a new payment?

13
00:00:42.476 --> 00:00:47.312
The card reader gives every payment a long unique code, called payment_uid.

14
00:00:47.312 --> 00:00:49.892
A copy sent again carries the same code.

15
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:55.708
A code like that is called an idempotency key: it lets the receiver spot a repeat.

16
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.548
So the obvious plan: look for the code.

17
00:00:58.548 --> 00:01:01.030
If it's not there, insert the payment.

18
00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:04.361
With one message at a time, this passes every test.

19
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.828
Picture two waiters and one paper order book.

20
00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:11.234
An order comes in twice, one copy to each waiter.

21
00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:12.693
Each checks the book.

22
00:01:12.693 --> 00:01:13.249
Nothing.

23
00:01:13.249 --> 00:01:15.473
Each walks off to write it down.

24
00:01:15.473 --> 00:01:18.671
Two plates, one table, one very full customer.

25
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:25.093
The waiters are the workers, the book is the payments table, checking is a look, and writing is an insert.

26
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:27.191
Let's slow our workers down.

27
00:01:27.191 --> 00:01:31.770
Worker A looks, worker B looks, and only then does either one insert.

28
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.429
What does each look find?

29
00:01:33.429 --> 00:01:34.491
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.826
Zero, for both.

31
00:01:38.826 --> 00:01:41.304
Neither has written anything yet.

32
00:01:41.304 --> 00:01:43.181
So both decide to insert.

33
00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:47.280
The answer was true when they asked, and out of date a moment later.

34
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.577
Something does stop it here.

35
00:01:49.577 --> 00:01:54.421
The payment_uid column is marked UNIQUE: no two rows may share a value.

36
00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:02.877
PostgreSQL keeps that rule with a unique index: a sorted list of every code in the column, and every insert must pass through it.

37
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:08.099
In the café, that's a book where checking and writing are one movement of one pen.

38
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:09.445
One more word.

39
00:02:09.445 --> 00:02:15.733
A transaction is a group of changes that the database applies all together, or not at all.

40
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.734
COMMIT saves them for good, and shows them to everyone else.

41
00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:21.352
ROLLBACK throws them away.

42
00:02:21.352 --> 00:02:25.087
Until a worker commits, no other worker can see its new row.

43
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:26.875
Now replay the race.

44
00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:28.100
Worker A inserts.

45
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:34.947
Its code goes into the unique index at once, marked as belonging to A's unfinished transaction.

46
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.024
Worker B's insert reaches the index and finds the same code.

47
00:02:39.024 --> 00:02:42.339
B can't know yet whether A will commit, so it waits.

48
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:43.947
A commits.

49
00:02:43.947 --> 00:02:49.885
B's insert fails: duplicate key value violates unique constraint.

50
00:02:49.885 --> 00:02:52.077
Its error code is 23505.

51
00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:56.280
Had A rolled back instead, B's insert would have gone through.

52
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.543
Either way, one row.

53
00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.184
So the data is safe.

54
00:02:59.184 --> 00:03:01.366
But catching that error is clumsy.

55
00:03:01.366 --> 00:03:04.704
An error spoils the whole transaction it happens in.

56
00:03:04.967 --> 00:03:11.372
A worker that stores 500 messages in one transaction loses all 500 to a single repeat.

57
00:03:11.372 --> 00:03:16.585
One extra coffee, and the whole morning's orders go back in the queue.

58
00:03:16.933 --> 00:03:23.865
Better to put the decision inside the insert itself: INSERT, ON CONFLICT on payment_uid, DO NOTHING.

59
00:03:23.865 --> 00:03:28.232
The insert asks the unique index in the same step as the write.

60
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.217
Replay it.

61
00:03:29.217 --> 00:03:34.522
A's insert goes in, and RETURNING hands back the new payment's number, 40.

62
00:03:34.522 --> 00:03:38.824
B's insert finds A's unfinished entry, and waits, as before.

63
00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:39.784
A commits.

64
00:03:39.784 --> 00:03:44.586
This time, B's insert sees the code is there, and skips the row.

65
00:03:44.586 --> 00:03:45.261
No error.

66
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:51.756
RETURNING gives B zero rows, which is how B's program knows the message was a repeat.

67
00:03:51.756 --> 00:03:52.639
One payment.

68
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.767
Here the pen picture breaks.

69
00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.913
A pen serves one writer at a time.

70
00:03:56.913 --> 00:04:01.584
PostgreSQL lets thousands of inserts with different codes through at once.

71
00:04:01.584 --> 00:04:04.867
Only inserts with the same code wait for each other.

72
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:10.340
And some second messages carry news, and should change the row instead of being skipped.

73
00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.769
That's the next section.

74
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:14.396
So how did one coffee get paid twice?

75
00:04:14.396 --> 00:04:19.096
Each worker looked, then wrote, and the look was out of date before the write.

76
00:04:19.096 --> 00:04:23.434
A check in a separate statement can be out of date before you act on it.

77
00:04:23.434 --> 00:04:25.121
Let the unique index decide.

78
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:26.471
One quick check.

79
00:04:26.471 --> 00:04:30.406
A's insert goes in, and then A crashes before it commits.

80
00:04:30.406 --> 00:04:31.303
B is waiting.

81
00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:34.962
How many payments end up stored, and who stores them?

82
00:04:34.962 --> 00:04:36.067
Pause and think.

83
00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:40.524
One, stored by B.

84
00:04:40.524 --> 00:04:44.739
A's unfinished entry vanishes, and B's insert goes ahead.

85
00:04:44.739 --> 00:04:48.289
Every order of events ends with exactly one row.
